  "slug": "acute-chronic-apoc1-f4a6",
  "summary": "Single-cell and multi-omics data are now routinely combined with Mendelian randomization and external cohorts to nominate candidate drivers across tissues.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is computational integration of single-cell and bulk transcriptomes with orthogonal layers, including Mendelian randomization, external cohorts, and experimental validation, to nominate specific cell subsets and programs. Several items revisit reference or classification choices: one colorectal cancer study examines the relationship between tumor-adjacent and distant normal mucosa, and a gastric tertiary lymphoid structure study defines a bulk-compatible signature across more than 1100 patients. Other items resolve cell states within diseased tissue, including altered cholangiocyte subsets in MASLD, an ARG1-associated PMN-MDSC-like network in multiple sclerosis bone marrow, and a CXCL1-associated fibroblast signature in deoxynivalenol-related barrier dysfunction. A shared macrophage inflammatory signature is proposed for MASLD and rheumatoid arthritis, and HDL-associated apolipoprotein M is prioritized in psoriasis. What is new is less a single mechanism than a repeatable workflow applied across organ systems.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-20"
}
